{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>This is the statute of the Torah which HaShem commanded.</b> When He said: הַזֵּה עֲלֵיהֶם מֵי חַטָּאת (Numbers 8:7). And so the Sages, of blessed memory, already said therefore concerning this that Scripture writes of it חֻקָּה — it is a decree from before Me and you have no permission to speculate after it, and Solomon said of it: אָמַרְתִּי אֶחְכָּמָה וְהִיא רְחוֹקָה מִמֶּנִּי (Ecclesiastes 7:23). Now the principal element of concealment in this matter is that it defiles the pure and purifies the defiled. Yet when we direct our understanding to the entire commandment, perhaps some subtle thing may be gleaned and taken from it. First: we find that all who are occupied with it, from the time of its burning onward, are impure — namely the one who burns it, the one who throws cedar wood and hyssop and scarlet wool into its burning, and the one who gathers and the one who touches and the one who carries. Yet the one who sprinkles and the one who sanctifies are pure. Second: the essential requirement of the cow is that it be red in perfection. And the prophet already explained that sin is likened to red, in his saying: אִם יִהְיוּ חֲטָאֵיכֶם כַּשָּׁנִים כַּשֶּׁלֶג יַלְבִּינוּ (Isaiah 1:18). Therefore the Sages, of blessed memory, said (Yoma 67a) that they would tie a scarlet thread to the opening of the Sanctuary — if it whitened, they rejoiced; if it did not whiten, they were saddened. Third: it is fitting to reflect that since every utterance of El is pure (Proverbs 30:5), without doubt it straightens deeds toward the mean, for each of the two extremes is repugnant, as it says: וְנֶעְקַשׁ דְּרָכַיִם יִפּוֹל בְּאֶחָת (Proverbs 28:18). Fourth: it is fitting to reflect that there is no proper way to straighten the crooked paths and restore them to the mean except by inclining them toward the opposite extreme, as happens in illnesses of the body, as it says: חַבּוּרוֹת פֶּצַע תַּמְרוּק בְּרָע (Proverbs 20:30) — even though that very inclining toward the extreme is itself repugnant and destructive to the condition of one whose way is meant to be balanced, like the effect of a purgative remedy that benefits the sick and harms the healthy. Fifth: it is fitting to reflect that the matter of the cow's ash has no role in purification from any species of impurity except purification from corpse-impurity. And it is already known that the matter of Torah and the commandments is life to those who find them (Proverbs 4:22) and to those who engage in them, as it says: כִּי הוּא חַיֵּיכֶם (Deuteronomy 32:47). And one who turns away from them toward the vanities of corruptible things goes toward death or is already fully dead, as they said (Berakhot 18a): \"The wicked, even in their lifetime, are called dead.\" Sixth: it is fitting to reflect on what the Sages, of blessed memory, said — that cedar wood alludes to arrogance and hyssop to its opposite, and since scarlet wool is with both of them, it alludes that both are sin, as they said (Arakhin 16b): בְּשַׁמְתָּא דְּאִית בֵּיהּ וּבְשַׁמְתָּא דְּלֵית בֵּיהּ — particularly for one who needs to conduct himself with leadership for the benefit of the public. And the Sages, of blessed memory, already said (Yoma 22b) that Saul was punished for not insisting on his honor, as it says: וַיְבַזֻּהוּ וְלֹא הֵבִיאוּ לוֹ מִנְחָה וַיְהִי כְּמַחֲרִישׁ (I Samuel 10:27), and so the prophet held him to account, saying: הֲלֹא אִם קָטֹן אַתָּה בְעֵינֶיךָ רֹאשׁ שִׁבְטֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל אָתָּה (I Samuel 15:17). Let us say, then: even though the commandment is a חֻקָּה and one must not speculate after it nor doubt whether it is fitting or not — for every utterance of El is pure and has a sublime reason without doubt, known to the King who commanded it and perhaps also to Moses our Master and those like him — nevertheless it contains some allusion to the way of repentance required of every sinner. And that is indeed an inclining toward the extreme opposite of his defiling deeds, which defile every pure heart, so that he may attain the way of the mean and become pure. And this way, though it is good and purifying for the sinner, is indeed blameworthy and evil and defiling to every pure heart, as they said (Ta'anit 11a): וְכִי בְּאֵיזֶה נֶפֶשׁ חָטָא זֶה, אֶלָּא שֶׁצִּעֵר עַצְמוֹ מִן הַיַּיִן (\"And in what way did this soul sin? Only that he afflicted himself from wine\"). Now the waters of purification, compounded from the ash of the burning and from water — which are two extremes from which the mean is renewed — allude that in the mean lies the repair of the sinner, which is called purity, as it says: מִכֹּל חַטֹּאתֵיכֶם לִפְנֵי ה׳ תִּטְהָרוּ (Leviticus 16:30). And among what is fitting to reflect upon is what the Decree-Giver decreed: that the one who touches a corpse defiles the Mishkan of HaShem, and the one who enters the tent defiles the Sanctuary of HaShem. For indeed, whoever draws near to the vanities of corruptible things undoubtedly defiles the pure heart, which is the dwelling of the Sanctuary of HaShem — which is called צֶלֶם אֱלֹהִים (the image of Elohim) — and his impurity is through the error of privation. And the one who enters the tent of the corpse, in the manner of שֹׁכְנֵי בָתֵּי חֹמֶר (Job 4:19), who have nothing before them but their body for a fleeting, unstable life — רַגְלֶיהָ יוֹרְדוֹת מָוֶת (Proverbs 5:5) — he undoubtedly defiles the Sanctuary of HaShem mentioned above, through which the people and the individual are to be holy to their Elohim, as it says: וְלִהְיוֹתְךָ עַם קָדוֹשׁ לַה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ (Deuteronomy 26:19). And then he defiles through the error of an acquisition contrary to what is intended. And therefore it is not fitting that tent-impurity apply except to the corpse of an Israelite, for indeed his matter alone is chosen and prepared above all others for the service of the blessed El; he alone sins through his noble faculty. But the sages who instruct sinners in the way — who are like the one who sprinkles and the one who sanctifies — no impurity whatsoever applies to them in this. And so, in the details of keeping this commandment as written and as received through tradition, there is an allusion to all these things, which are undoubtedly the intentions of the Torah."], :book :numbers, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "The Aramaic phrase בְּשַׁמְתְתָּא דְּאִית בֵּיהּ וּבְשַׁמְתָּא דְּלֵית בֵּיהּ (Arakhin 16b) refers to a tallit that has the requisite tsitsit threads and one that lacks them; Sforno cites it to illustrate arrogance with and without justification. The Ta'anit phrase is left in its original Aramaic as Sforno quotes it. טָעוּת הֶעְדֵּרִי and טָעוּת קִנְיָנִי הֶפְכִּי are rendered as 'error of privation' and 'error of an acquisition contrary to what is intended,' preserving Sforno's philosophical terminology for types of cognitive error."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:40:48.046109207Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}